at her family home in county dunny goal  kerry frye is trying to prepare for a  new term at university in dublin but  after four months of searching is still  without a place to rent term starts in  about a week and a half and nowhere to  live nowhere to go no one will take  students with the term so close it's  just  very terrifying honestly  kerry has even seen fraudulent listings  trying to scam students out of deposits  amid this rent crisis irish police say  such crime has increased by 30 percent  since before the pandemic they probably  are desperate yet but they're also  seeing it as a one-time offer you know  it's been offered in deceit on social  media this is one chance you must take  it today there's a sense of urgency if i  don't take it i'm going to lose it  construction shut down during covet and  simply cannot meet demand private  landlords have left the market in droves  driven off by high tax and rent controls  it's led to scenes like this the queues  for a viewing of just one rental  property in the capital recently  from donegal to dublin the rental crisis  is touching every part of ireland  nationwide a new low was reached last  month when according to one leading  property website in a country of more  than 5 million people there were just  over 700 rental properties available  anywhere in ireland  after five years courtney doyle has  finally found a place to rent with help  from a government scheme it's urgently  needed as she's six months pregnant  it must mean worrying yeah at the time  that you fell apart  you had no home no i mean it caused a  lot of stress some anxiety you know  especially like celtic and i was like  what if i don't find a place on time is  it suitable to go ahead with a pregnancy  is it fair but there's just not enough  houses for us or they're just not  affordable you know especially for  people who have lower income  the government can't hide the scale of  the problem how chronic is ireland's  rental crisis  look it's accused there is no question  of that  we've had an issue of under supply  across all 10 years of housing for the  last 10 years  there's no question and that has made  the situation much more difficult the  government has launched a 4 billion euro  a year housing for all plan to try and  tackle the problem but for now all over  ireland the search for somewhere to rent  remains frustrating and fruitless  stephen murphy sky news
 

